summary:
Each grill question becomes a set of radio buttons: the choices plus Other. Picking one opens a single text box under it. The box is optional for a choice and required for Other, and the text moves along when the owner switches options. The separate own-answer and ask-back boxes go. An Other answer is a new answer kind, 'other'. On submit it goes into the question's thread, the way an ask back does today, and the question stays open until Claude either records a decision for it (which closes it, with the owner's text as the answer) or replies (it stays open and the owner picks again). After 3 replies, Claude has to settle the question with a decision; `grill pending` marks it `must_settle`, and the page's hint says the Other text now counts as the answer. The server no longer accepts own or ask submissions or drafts, but grill.json files from 0.1.9 that contain them still load and show. The final step becomes Yes (confirms at once) or No (a required box, then Send reopens the grill). The rest of the grill page gets one clean look:
- answered questions are tidy rows with a tick, the answer in bold and the note in grey;
- each round has a heading;
- every text box has a hint inside it;
- the decisions list sits in a sticky side column that can be collapsed (the state is remembered in the browser), grouped by topic, with each decision's why shown and replaced decisions behind 'Show replaced (N)';
- Claude's banner sticks to the top of the window on the grill page and the plan page, below the theme switch.

/ship's page grill instructions and the README describe the new controls (plan-review goes to 0.1.11), and /ship tells Claude to keep each decision to one line. examples/grill.json becomes a demo in the new format with an open round, and examples/grill-done.json shows the Yes/No step. CONTRIBUTING.md has the commands to open either one, so the owner can check the finished page in the browser before the commit.

files.reason:
The grill page's look:
- the header with room under the top bar;
- two columns with a sticky, scrollable decisions aside whose top sits below the banner's height, so the two never overlap;
- aligned radio options with full-width boxes;
- answered rows in the page font, left aligned and separated, with round headings;
- a Yes/No row of matching buttons.
The sticky .claude-banner gets a solid background (both pages). Light and dark come through the existing variables.
